gary", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "treaty-of-london", "targetName": "Treaty of London", "context": "The Treaty of London aimed to succeed in undermining Austria-Hungary by promising Italy significant territorial gains."}], "places": [{"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Signing location"}, {"name": "Vienna, Austria", "role": "Diplomatic context"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Government seat"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Treaty of London", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "treaty-of-london"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q824165", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_London_(1915)"], "tags": ["United Kingdom", "treaty", "Europe", "International Treaties & Conventions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Treaty of London contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped diplomatic relations and territorial boundaries. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Treaty of London played a crucial role in Italy's participation in World War I, which had profound implications for post-war Europe and the rise of fascism, affecting millions and shaping the continent's future.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T13:11:04Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#29922158072", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Secret pact between the triple entente and italy, signed in london on 26 april 1915. Treaty of London is a treaty from Europe. A moderately significant entity in the historical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "The Treaty of London, signed on April 26, 1915, stands as a pivotal agreement in the context of World War I, orchestrated between the Allies, primarily the Triple Entente, and Italy. This treaty marked Italy's official entry into the war on the side of the Allies and represented a strategic shift in the balance of power in Europe. Key figures involved included Italian Prime Minister Vittorio Emanuele Orlando and representatives from the United Kingdom, France, and Russia. The treaty promised Italy territorial gains at the expense of Austria-Hungary, thus not only altering the military landscape but also setting the stage for future geopolitical tensions in the region.

The core events leading to the Treaty of London were rooted in Italy's desire to expand its territory and influence in the Balkans and Adriatic Sea. The negotiations culminated in the agreement whereby Italy would join the Allies against the Central Powers in exchange for promises of land, including parts of Tyrol, the Dalmatian coast, and territories inhabited by Italians. The signing of this treaty was shrouded in secrecy, reflecting the complex diplomatic maneuvers of the time and the precarious nature of alliances during wartime.

